// The Wells Over the Buried Lake. On a dry ochre plain a green city stands exactly as far as a hidden lake reaches
// under the rock: its edge is the lake's shore (ONE curve, makeLake in the part file: the two outlines are one list).
// Over its flat roofs a second city of lifting machines: well heads with buckets, windmills with cloth sails, great
// water wheels, pump beams, drill trestles, tanks on legs, weathercocks; and a well in every yard the lake's roof
// allows: the great kinds by quarter, and a small one (a kerb, a post, a pulley, a rope) wherever a yard is left.
//
// THE CITY HAS A PLAN OF ITS OWN, made before any route exists and reading none: the gate on the south shore, the
// court of the great well with THE HEAD TOWER over it (the town's one landmark: great timbers on stone piers, a
// crown to 53 m, the lifting wheel 38 m across beside it), and between them the main street (its own curve, dealt),
// the two aqueducts each on its own course from its wheel at the gate to its tank by the court (slender piers, high
// arches, the water running along the top), a side lane that leaves the street under one arch, runs behind the
// first row of houses and across the MARKET YARD (the widest yard: stalls, a tank on legs spilling into a runnel,
// broad sure wells), and a second lane back to the street's last straight: the lanes, the arch and the yard are
// dealt on the town's own stream from the town's measures. Houses stand on lots of that plan, gardens between them
// (mounded beds of vegetables, trellised vines, fruit trees, small tiled pools with their runnels, garden walls,
// jars), the whole town alike. THE GREEN ENDS WHERE THE LAKE ENDS: outside the lake's outline nothing is planted,
// and nothing is watered there BUT AT THE GATE: each of the two gate wheels stands at the edge's foot in A KERBED
// BASIN of its own, built across the outline: one sheet of water 22.5 m long and 9.8 m across inside a raised kerb
// of pale stone (0.5 m broad, 0.8 m high), the wheel in its inner half with its axle 3.5 m beyond the edge at its
// own place (1.8 to 3.5 m from the outline itself), the water reaching 10.7 to 15.8 m outside the outline and the
// kerb 16.6 m at the most (by count on seeds 1 to 300; the plain's own rocks, thorn and scrub keep off the basins).
// Every palm is rooted inside the outline, and on seeds 1 to 40 about one palm in ten, 6 to 24 a seed, has a frond's tip over the edge's kerb, most by under 2 m, 4.3 m at the most.
// Before the gate lies the dry plain (rocks, a few thorn trees and low scrub, the plain's own dry growth, dun) and the road of beaten earth, with a few
// walkers and donkeys under water jars on it.
// Nothing is dealt, thinned, turned or made finer by the route. One thing only is asked of the line: what would
// stand too near it is left out, its draws made all the same. WHAT EACH KIND IS ASKED: every kind but one keeps the
// lane's true clearance, 2.55 m, between the line and its own drawn extent (a post's girth, a crown's round, a
// hull's box, a glow's half breadth, the sails' round and the tower of the town's own mills; a mooring's pile by
// the round that holds its timber, its plain iron reaching 0.12 m past that round: said at the moorings); THE
// LANES' MILLS ARE ASKED MORE, AND THIS ONE CLEARANCE IS THE LINE'S: a lane mill's whole round and its tower are
// kept off the FLOWN LINE by the sails' own reach (4.25 to 7.1 m), more than the lane's 2.55 m that every other
// kind keeps: a keeping off of a turning body by place and by the mill's own measure. IT IS MEASURED FROM THE LINE
// (the line's table) AND NOT FROM THE LANE. By the reader's count, with a count-only copy, it leaves out 228 of
// 1,730 lane mills on seeds 1 to 300, and it leaves seed 264 none of its three. No other placing reads the route's table:
// the lanes' mills keep THE COURT'S ROUND by the plan alone (none within 76 m of the great well's axis, on every
// bearing alike).
// THE ROUTE IS LAID AFTERWARD FROM THE PLAN'S PLACES:
// 57 to 63 m of the plain's road to the gate between the two wheels; 20 m and more of the street; onto the
// side lane and under the arch; up over the roofs of the quarter and across the market yard; back over the top of
// the same aqueduct (the other is seen across the street from there) to the court from its side; onto the court's
// axis; down the great well in one straight dive between the chains of pots (the line 59 degrees at its steepest, the lens 58);
// out under the rock into the great well's chamber; ONE TURN there of 201 to 206 degrees (a half round and a little
// more, out toward the chamber's rim and back: it is not a round about the column) onto the nave's line; past the
// great shaft's column of light 30 m abeam; then the nave to the landing.
//
// THE CAVERN'S PLAN IS THE HALL'S OWN (the 'chamber', 'nave', 'cave' and 'aisles' streams; no line is read). Under the
// great shaft the rock opens into THE GREAT WELL'S CHAMBER: one round chamber about the shaft's axis, 63 to 67 m in
// radius (dealt), domed, a ring of piers of the living rock at its rim and no pier within it; the shaft's daylight
// lights the whole of it. THE NAVE passes the shaft 29.5 to 32.5 m to one hand (dealt) and runs to the far shore:
// beyond the chamber two rows of great piers of the living rock about 23 to 26 m apart, THE ROWS WANDERING AND THE
// BAYS UNEQUAL (a pier every 0.72 to 1.35 of a mean bay of 19 to 23 m, the two rows not opposite one another, one
